<h1>Jaxon Smith-Njigba's Hot Start: <em>The Test He Skipped Explains It</em></h1>
<div class="meta-row">
<span>📖 5 min read</span>
<span>🏈 Football</span>
<span>🔥 Seahawks · Week 3</span>
</div>
<p class="dek">Jaxon Smith-Njigba has 17 catches for 277 yards and 4 touchdowns through two games, picking up right where his league-leading, Offensive Player of the Year 2025 season left off. He never ran a 40-yard dash at his own combine — but the test he did run explains exactly why his game has translated so cleanly to the pros.</p>
</div><div class="content">
<div class="toc">
<div class="toc-label">In This Article</div>
<a href="#start">The Hot Start</a>
<a href="#combine">The Combine He Skipped</a>
<a href="#why">Why It Predicted This</a>
<a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
</div>
<h2 id="start">The Hot Start</h2>
<p>Smith-Njigba opened his 2026 season with an 82-yard touchdown on Seattle's first drive of Week 1, then added three more touchdowns in Week 2 against Arizona — a 7-yard score in the third quarter and a 12-yard score in the fourth. Across two games he's turned 22 targets into 17 catches, 277 yards, and 4 touchdowns, and he'll get a favorable matchup in Week 3 against a Washington secondary that's already given up big plays early this season.</p>
<div class="stat-card">
<div class="stat-icon">🎯</div>
<div class="stat-title">Jaxon Smith-Njigba — Through 2 Games</div>
<div class="stat-sub">Seattle Seahawks · Reigning NFL Offensive Player of the Year</div>
<div class="stat-numbers">
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">17/22</div><div class="label">Catches / Targets</div></div>
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">277</div><div class="label">Receiving Yards</div></div>
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">4</div><div class="label">Touchdowns</div></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>This isn't a hot streak out of nowhere. Smith-Njigba led the entire NFL with 1,793 receiving yards in 2025 — the eighth-most in a single season in NFL history — while pacing all receivers in target share, air yards, and yards per route run. He was ranked No. 5 on the league's Top 100 list heading into this season, and he's making an early case to climb higher.</p>
<h2 id="combine">The Combine Test He Skipped</h2>
<p>Here's what makes Smith-Njigba's profile unusual: he never ran the 40-yard dash at the 2023 NFL Combine. He was returning from a hamstring injury that limited him to three games as a junior at Ohio State, and rather than risk a setback, he chose to skip the marquee speed test entirely, planning to run it at his Pro Day instead — which he did, clocking a solid but unspectacular 4.52.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Test</th><th>Result</th><th>Field Context</th></tr>
<tr><td>40-Yard Dash</td><td>4.52 (Pro Day only)</td><td>Solid, not elite</td></tr>
<tr><td>3-Cone Drill</td><td>6.57s</td><td>Fastest of any WR, ~0.3s clear of 2nd</td></tr>
<tr><td>20-Yard Shuttle</td><td>3.93s</td><td>Fastest of any WR, ~0.2s clear of 2nd</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vertical Jump</td><td>35"</td><td>Solid for the position</td></tr>
<tr><td>Broad Jump</td><td>10'5"</td><td>Solid for the position</td></tr>
</table>
<p>What he did run at the combine was more telling than the 40 would have been anyway. His 6.57-second 3-cone drill was nearly three-tenths of a second faster than any other receiver in a stacked 2023 class — a gap that large in change-of-direction testing is rare. His 20-yard shuttle told the same story: 3.93 seconds, again the fastest of any wideout, again by close to two-tenths of a second over the field.</p>
