<h2 id="why">Why It Predicted This</h2>
<p>Straight-line speed and route-running are two different skills, and NFL scouting has historically overweighted the 40-yard dash relative to how much it actually predicts receiver success. The 3-cone and shuttle measure something more directly relevant to what a slot and possession receiver does on every snap: the ability to plant, cut, and accelerate out of a break faster than the defender covering him. That's precisely the tool set that turns a crisp route into consistent, high-value separation — and it's exactly what's shown up on tape as Smith-Njigba has become one of the league's most productive receivers regardless of who's throwing him the ball.</p>
<p>It's also worth noting how little his production has depended on quarterback continuity. Seattle has cycled through different starters across the past two seasons, and Smith-Njigba has kept producing at an elite level regardless — a mark of a receiver whose game is built on separation ability rather than one specific rapport.</p>
<div class="callout">
<strong>The Lesson in the Numbers</strong>
  A 40 time gets the headlines, but for a route-runner like Smith-Njigba, the 3-cone and shuttle were the tests that actually mattered. Elite change-of-direction speed, not straight-line speed, is what's separating him from press coverage on 82-yard scores and short-area touchdowns alike.
</div>
